# Language Policy

## Instruction documents (English)

All content that instructs the agent must be in **English**:

- `.cursor/rules/**/*.mdc` — rule body and structure
- `.cursor/commands/**/*.md` — slash command text
- `.cursor/skills/**/SKILL.md` — skill instructions
- `.cursor/agents/**/*.mdc` — agent role and constraints

Rationale: instruction-following and token efficiency tend to be more reliable when the instruction layer is in English. All agent prompts (`docs/cursor-subagents/*.md`, `.cursor/agents/*.mdc`) and workflow docs they reference (`docs/workflow/CURSOR-SUBAGENTS-DESIGN.md`, `AGENT-COLLABORATION-ON-REQUIREMENT.md`) are in English so that handoffs and directions are clear. The model still outputs in the user's language when the user requests it.

## User-facing response (requested language)

- **User-facing replies** (explanations, summaries, error messages, suggestions) MUST be in the **user's requested language** (e.g. Korean if the user asked in Korean or set "Always respond in 한국어").
- Code, file paths, identifiers, and technical terms may stay as-is; only the surrounding natural language follows the user's language.

## Instruction → output split

- **Instructions** (rules, commands, skills, agent prompts): English so that direction is clear and consistent for the model.
- **Output to the user**: In the **user's requested language** (e.g. Korean when the user writes in Korean or has "Always respond in 한국어"). Code, file paths, and technical identifiers stay as-is; only the surrounding explanations, summaries, and messages follow the user's language.

## What stays language-agnostic or mixed

- File paths, repo structure, and doc references: no change.
- User-facing docs (e.g. `docs/requirements/*.md`, README) may use the project's usual language; instruction documents in `.cursor/` follow the rule above.
